@page "/"

<PageTitle>AccessToBlazer</PageTitle>

<h1>Access to Blazor sample</h1>

<p>
    This is a small, self-contained demonstration of porting a Microsoft Access
    database to an <strong>ASP.NET Core Blazor Server</strong> application.
</p>

<div class="row mt-4">
    <div class="col-lg-7">
        <h2>What was ported</h2>
        <p>
            The original Access file (<code>docs/AccessToBlazerSample.accdb</code>) contains
            three tables and three bound forms, with <em>no authentication</em>:
        </p>
        <table class="table">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Access form</th>
                    <th>Access table</th>
                    <th>Blazor page</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Products</td>
                    <td>Products</td>
                    <td><a href="products">/products</a></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Customers</td>
                    <td>Customers</td>
                    <td><a href="customers">/customers</a></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Orders (combo lookups)</td>
                    <td>Orders</td>
                    <td><a href="orders">/orders</a></td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <div class="col-lg-5">
        <h2>How it maps</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Access <em>table</em> &rarr; EF Core entity + SQL Server table</li>
            <li>Access <em>form</em> &rarr; Razor component (Blazor Server page)</li>
            <li>Access <em>combo box</em> &rarr; <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> bound to a lookup list</li>
            <li>Access &ldquo;create / edit / browse&rdquo; &rarr; inline editor with <code>EditForm</code></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-muted">
            The database is created and seeded automatically on first run against
            SQL Server LocalDB.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>